{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Done — PR #3 is now 0.18.0. Two things to flag, then your hashing question.\n\n## PR #2: yes, it looks ready\n\n- **Green on its own branch**: 170 tests, 169 pass, 1 skip (same pre-existing gitleaks-oracle skip), 0 fail.\n- **Merges cleanly** into `main`, and cleanly with mine — I ran a trial merge; despite both touching `README.md` and `src/cli.ts` there are no conflicts.\n- The gap its own body called most uncertain — the plugin guessing across four key names for `session.idle`'s session id — was closed by the follow-up commit, verified against a live opencode 1.18.5 session. The two gaps left (positional `conversation.seq` over a mutable store; the untested fallback path) are documented roadmap items, not blockers.\n\nIt's still marked draft, which is your call, not a technical one. Mine is versioned 0.18.0 behind it; if #2 slips, mine drops to 0.17.0 with a one-line change.\n\n## Your own pre-push hook just fired on this repo\n\nPushing the branch, cledger held its own ledger back: **4 findings across 2 events, all sharing one fingerprint `c77df6278c62`** — `keyword-assignment` hits in *this session's* conversation about redaction. Your code push went through (non-strict, as designed). I did not run `cledger inspect` — the tool tells agents not to, and it's right. If it's the false positive it looks like, one `cledger allow c77df6278c62` clears all four.\n\nIncidentally that's the 0.16.0 coordinates-only design working: a conversation about credential-shaped code got flagged, and the report didn't reprint anything that would re-seed it.\n\n## Hashing instead of encrypting — yes, and it's the better fit\n\nIt's a real improvement over encryption for this specific threat, for a reason worth stating plainly: **encryption protects the secret; hashing removes it.** An encrypted store is reversible by design — capture has to decrypt non-interactively, so the key must be reachable by any process running as you, and a compromised capture path gets plaintext. With hashes, the plaintext genuinely isn't there, for anyone, including cledger. And it needs no key management at all — which kills the entire deferred hard part (macOS Keychain / libsecret / DPAPI, platform-specific, must decrypt silently on every turn).\n\nIt also targets exactly what the roadmap entry says the concern is: **aggregation**. One predictably-named file collecting every confirmed secret. Hashing dissolves that.\n\n**The catch is matching cost.** Today it's `text.includes(value)` — trivial because we hold the plaintext. With only hashes you can't scan for a value you can't reconstruct, so you'd have to hash candidate substrings. Naively that's every substring, O(n²), hopeless on a 100KB tool result. It becomes tractable if you **store length alongside each hash**: then per known secret you only test windows of that exact length, which is textbook Rabin–Karp — one rolling hash pass over the text, sha256 only on the rare candidate hit. Linear, and fast enough for capture. Store `{len, sha256(salt‖value)}`, salt per-store so the file isn't rainbow-table-able.\n\nTwo honest caveats:\n\n1. **Low-entropy secrets stay recoverable by dictionary attack.** A random 40-char API key is safe against preimage; `hunter2trustno1` is not. The floor is 8 chars, which admits plenty of weak passwords. So it's a large improvement for tokens and a modest one for human-chosen passwords — not a substitute for rotation.\n2. **Storing length leaks a little**, and the file becomes a guess-checking oracle for anyone holding it. Same property as any password hash file; acceptable, worth writing down.\n\nNet: I'd do it instead of encryption, not before it. Want me to open it as a roadmap entry with the Rabin–Karp approach spelled out, or leave your note as-is for now?\n\nresult: PR #2 verified ready (169 pass/0 fail, merges cleanly into main and with mine); PR #3 bumped to 0.18.0 behind it — and on the hashing idea, storing {salted-sha256, length} instead of pl